@flexiarg or3/authorship-for-first-time-contributors
@title Authorship for First-Time Contributors
@audience PIs, paper leads, research culture leads
@tone formal-analytic
@style design-pattern
@sigils [✍️/名 🚪/入]
@see or3/student-project-as-collaboration, or/attribution-forward-review

! conclusion: Authorship for First-Time Contributors

  + context: The people who made the work possible are not the people who usually appear on the paper.

  + IF:
    Your project depends on contributions from actors, practitioners, community partners, technicians, or students — people who shaped what was done but have never been an author on anything.

  + HOWEVER:
    Authorship conventions quietly select for those who can attend the writing meetings and edit the draft. Contributors who cannot are moved to the acknowledgements, and the credit for a collaborative piece of work accrues to the academics who wrote it up.

  + THEN:
    Decide authorship on contribution to the work rather than contribution to the manuscript, and make participation possible in whatever form suits each contributor — a recorded conversation, a reviewed summary, comments relayed by a colleague — rather than requiring everyone to edit the same document. Say at the outset that first-time authors are expected, and walk them through what authorship commits them to.

  + BECAUSE:
    A named author has a citable record, a reason to stay involved, and standing in the next project; an acknowledged helper has none of these. For non-academic contributors this is often the only durable return they receive, and it costs the project nothing but attention.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Apply a contribution taxonomy (e.g. CRediT) early, while roles can still be shaped, rather than at submission.]
    next[Ask contributors how they would like to be named, including whether they want to be.]
